-- title:
--   Branches of a Drinfeld-type plane curve singularity
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime and $k$ a field in which $q = 0$. Let $f$ be a formal power series in two variables $X_0, X_1$ over $k$, i.e. an element of `MvPowerSeries (Fin 2) k`, and suppose that $f - (X_0 X_1^q - X_0^q X_1)$ lies in the $(q+2)$-nd power of the ideal generated by $X_0$ and $X_1$; that is, $f \equiv X_0 X_1^q - X_0^q X_1$ modulo $(X_0, X_1)^{q+2}$. Let $a \in k$ satisfy $a^q = a$. Then there exists a one-variable formal power series $\varphi \in k[[T]]$ whose constant coefficient vanishes and whose coefficient of $T^1$ equals $a$, such that substituting $X_0 \mapsto T$ and $X_1 \mapsto \varphi$ into $f$ yields $0$; the substitution is the `MvPowerSeries.subst` of the pair $![X, \varphi]$ into $f$, which is legitimate because both entries have zero constant term. Thus the curve $f = 0$ has a formal branch through the origin tangent to the line $X_1 = a X_0$, for each of the $q$ roots $a$ of $a^q = a$ in $k$.
--
--   The series $X_0 X_1^q - X_0^q X_1$ is the local equation at a supersingular point of the Drinfeld level structure moduli problem, and the statement exhibits its $q$ formal branches, one for each $a \in \mathbb{F}_q \subseteq k$, for any series congruent to it to order $q+2$. It is used to show that the quotient of the two-variable power series ring by such an $f$ is reduced, via [`MvPowerSeries.isReduced_quotient_span_of_sub_drinfeld_mem_pow`](thm.html#MvPowerSeries.isReduced_quotient_span_of_sub_drinfeld_mem_pow).
-- source:
